```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
The BGP daemon (bgpd) on leaf_router_0_1 has crashed and failed to restart.
FRR's watchdog process (watchfrr) is also in a FAILED state on the same
device, which means there is no automatic recovery mechanism. The underlying
configuration file (/etc/frr/frr.conf) is intact and correct — it configures
leaf_router_0_1 as AS 65201 with router-id 172.16.0.7, advertises
10.0.1.0/24, and peers with both spines (172.16.0.6 / AS 65100 and
172.16.0.10 / AS 65101). The /etc/frr/daemons file also correctly enables
bgpd. Because bgpd is not running, leaf_router_0_1 never establishes its BGP
sessions: both spine_router_0_0 and spine_router_0_1 show their sessions
toward leaf_router_0_1's addresses (172.16.0.7, 172.16.0.11) permanently
stuck in the Active state. As a result, the prefix 10.0.1.0/24 is never
injected into the fabric — neither spine, the super-spine, nor leaf_router_0_0
carry a route to it. When pc_0_0 sends traffic to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2),
leaf_router_0_0 has no matching route and returns an ICMP "Destination Net
Unreachable," which is exactly the observed symptom. pc_0_1's NIC (eth0) and
cabling are completely healthy — the interface is UP/UP with zero errors and
the correct IP address. The team's hypothesis of a bad NIC or loose cable is
incorrect. The fix is to restart the FRR service on leaf_router_0_1
(`service frr restart`), which will bring bgpd back up with the existing
valid configuration and re-establish both spine BGP sessions.
=== END ===
```